3. Commercial evaluation

Each Organization is permitted one evaluation period of 30 consecutive calendar
days, beginning with that Organization's first commercial evaluation use of the
Harness. Evaluation is permitted solely to determine the Harness's suitability
for adoption. The Licensor may extend the period in writing.

Controlled and common-control affiliates are part of the same Organization.
Reinstalling, downloading again, modifying, forking, changing employees or teams,
moving use to an affiliate, or testing another runtime does not reset or renew
the period. Evaluation periods may not be repeatedly recreated through employees,
affiliates, contractors, engagements, or intermediaries.

A consultant or service provider may assist a client during that client's valid
evaluation period. Ongoing paid use for client services requires commercial
licensing; changing consultants or engagements does not restart evaluation.

4. Commercial licensing

After the evaluation period, a separate commercial license is required for
ongoing commercial activity involving the Harness, including:

- recurring commercial development or testing;
- automated or recurring CI/CD;
- release qualification or release gating;
- testing used to develop, maintain, validate, or improve commercial products
  or services;
- production validation;
- ongoing internal commercial agent or governance testing;
- commercial security, assurance, or compliance work;
- paid consulting or professional services;
- customer testing or reporting;
- hosted testing or governance-validation services;
- embedding the Harness in another commercial product, platform, or tool;
- commercial redistribution; and
- other operational commercial reliance.

During evaluation, commercial use must still be solely for the purpose stated
in Section 3. Neither evaluation nor this section grants redistribution rights
otherwise withheld by Section 7.

Noncommercial open-source projects qualify for free use. Commercial organizations
developing, supporting, hosting, or selling open-source products follow the same
commercial-use rules as other commercial organizations.

The rules apply neutrally whether the runtime tested is CDE/Kingpin, a competing
system, or an internal system. Commercial terms are arranged separately; no
